  "enhanced_information": "The patient's symptoms of pruritus, jaundice, and hepatosplenomegaly, combined with a history of ulcerative colitis, strongly suggest primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC). Standard medical consensus indicates that MRCP is the preferred non-invasive diagnostic tool for PSC, as it can visualize bile duct strictures characteristic of the disease. While ERCP can also show bile duct strictures, it is more invasive and typically used for therapeutic purposes rather than initial diagnosis. Liver biopsy can show 'onion-skin' fibrosis, but it is not the first-line diagnostic test due to its invasive nature. Ultrasound and contrast CT may show bile duct abnormalities but are less specific for PSC. Therefore, MRCP is the most appropriate test to confirm the diagnosis of PSC in this patient, aligning with the consensus that it is the best non-invasive method to visualize the characteristic bile duct changes.",
  "analysis": "The patient's symptoms of pruritus, jaundice, and hepatosplenomegaly, along with a history of ulcerative colitis, are highly suggestive of primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC). PSC is commonly associated with inflammatory bowel diseases like ulcerative colitis. The most effective non-invasive diagnostic tool for PSC is Magnetic Resonance Cholangiopancreatography (MRCP), as it can visualize the characteristic bile duct strictures and irregularities. While Endoscopic Retrograde Cholangiopancreatography (ERCP) can also diagnose PSC, it is more invasive and typically reserved for therapeutic interventions. A liver biopsy can show specific histological features but is not the first-line diagnostic test due to its invasive nature. Ultrasound and contrast CT are less specific for PSC. Therefore, MRCP is the most appropriate and consensus-supported test to confirm the diagnosis of PSC in this patient.",
